            "passage": "<b>THE POWER OF SHARED FAMILY MEALS</b>\nSharing a meal seems too ordinary to deserve attention, yet a growing body of research suggests otherwise. According to The Family Dinner Project, studies link regular family dinners with healthier eating habits, higher self-esteem, stronger resilience and better grades. Conversation around the table even seems to build children’s vocabulary, and shared meals are also linked with improved mental health and stronger relationships. Put simply, the dinner table is where family members feed both their bodies and their relationships.\nDespite these benefits, family meals are becoming less common. Civil Eats reports that <u><b>they</b></u> have declined by more than thirty per cent over the past three decades, and that only about a quarter of families with children eat together seven or more times a week. <u><b>Busy schedules, long commutes and conflict inside the family can all prevent people from eating together.</b></u> Even when people do sit at the same table, screens may replace conversation.\nThe good news is that a family dinner does not have to be perfect. The Family Dinner Project stresses that its goals are about real life, not perfection: <u><b>regular</b></u> dinners with food, fun and conversation about things that matter. Families can start by cooking together, playing simple games at the table and using conversation starters to get everyone talking. Free recipes, games and tools are available on the project’s website. One of its founders adds that children who know their family’s stories become more resilient and have higher self-esteem.\nOf course, no single dinner will solve every family problem, and some families simply cannot eat together every night. That is not a reason to give up. Even one or two shared meals a week can create moments of connection, and the <u><b>habit</b></u> can grow slowly. What matters most is not the food on the plate but the attention people give one another while they eat.\n<i>(Adapted from The Family Dinner Project – thefamilydinnerproject.org, and Civil Eats – “The Family Dinner Project Wants to Help You Make Communal Meals Happen”)</i>"
